const pythonLessons=[
{title:'Python Introduction',level:'Beginner',intro:'Python is a high-level, readable programming language used for automation, websites, data analysis, artificial intelligence, scripting, and education. A Python program is made from instructions that the interpreter executes.',sections:[['Why learn Python?','Python has simple syntax, a large standard library, and a huge ecosystem. It lets beginners focus on problem-solving instead of complicated syntax.'],['Your first program','The print() function displays information in the console.'],['Code example',`print("Hello, CodewithHafiz!")\nprint("I am learning Python.")`]],output:`Hello, CodewithHafiz!\nI am learning Python.`,practice:'Print your name, city, and one goal you have for learning Python.',mistakes:'Remember that text must be inside quotes. Python is case-sensitive: print() is correct, Print() is not.'},
{title:'Installing Python and Running Code',level:'Beginner',intro:'To write Python programs, install Python from python.org or use an editor such as VS Code. Python files normally use the .py extension.',sections:[['Check the installation','Open a terminal and run python --version. On some Windows systems, use py --version.'],['Running a file','Save your file as hello.py, then run python hello.py in the same folder.'],['Code example',`# hello.py\nmessage = "Python is ready"\nprint(message)`]],output:'Python is ready',practice:'Create a file named practice.py and run a program that prints three lines.',mistakes:'Make sure the file is saved with .py, not .py.txt. If python is not recognized, reinstall Python and enable Add Python to PATH.'},
{title:'Comments and Code Formatting',level:'Beginner',intro:'Comments explain code for humans and are ignored by Python. Good formatting makes programs easier to read and maintain.',sections:[['Single-line comments','Start a comment with #. Everything after # on that line is ignored.'],['Readable formatting','Use meaningful names, indentation, and blank lines to separate ideas.'],['Code example',`# Store the learner's name\nstudent_name = "Hafiz"\n\n# Display a welcome message\nprint("Welcome", student_name)`]],output:'Welcome Hafiz',practice:'Write a small program with two comments and three readable variable names.',mistakes:'Do not use comments as a replacement for clear code. Keep indentation consistent.'},
{title:'Variables and Assignment',level:'Beginner',intro:'A variable is a name that refers to a value. Python creates a variable when you assign a value with the = operator. You can later assign a new value to the same name.',sections:[['Assignment','The expression on the right is evaluated and stored under the name on the left.'],['Naming rules','Names can contain letters, numbers, and underscores, but cannot start with a number. Avoid Python keywords.'],['Code example',`name = "Hafiz"\nage = 18\nage = age + 1\nprint(name)\nprint(age)`]],output:'Hafiz\n19',practice:'Create variables for a product name, price, and quantity, then print them.',mistakes:'= assigns a value; == compares values. Use descriptive names such as total_price instead of x.'},
{title:'Data Types',level:'Beginner',intro:'A data type describes the kind of value stored in a variable. Common built-in types include strings, integers, floats, booleans, lists, tuples, dictionaries, and sets.',sections:[['Common types','str stores text, int stores whole numbers, float stores decimal numbers, and bool stores True or False.'],['Checking a type','Use type(value) to inspect the type of a value.'],['Code example',`name = "Hafiz"\nage = 18\nheight = 1.72\nis_student = True\n\nprint(type(name))\nprint(type(age))\nprint(type(height))\nprint(type(is_student))`]],output:`<class 'str'>\n<class 'int'>\n<class 'float'>\n<class 'bool'>`,practice:'Create one variable of each of the four basic types and print its type.',mistakes:'True and False must start with capital letters. A number inside quotes is text, not an integer.'},
{title:'Input and Output',level:'Beginner',intro:'The input() function receives text from a user. The print() function displays values. Input always returns a string, so convert it when you need a number.',sections:[['Getting input','input("Prompt") pauses the program and returns what the user types.'],['Converting input','Use int() for whole numbers and float() for decimal numbers.'],['Code example',`name = input("Enter your name: ")\nage = int(input("Enter your age: "))\nprint(f"Hello {name}, you are {age} years old.")`]],output:'Enter your name: Hafiz\nEnter your age: 18\nHello Hafiz, you are 18 years old.',practice:'Ask the user for two numbers and print their sum.',mistakes:'Calling int() on non-numeric text raises ValueError. Always use clear prompts.'},
{title:'Operators',level:'Beginner',intro:'Operators perform calculations, comparisons, and logical operations. Arithmetic operators include +, -, *, /, //, %, and **.',sections:[['Arithmetic','/ returns normal division, // returns floor division, % returns a remainder, and ** raises a power.'],['Comparison','==, !=, >, <, >=, and <= produce boolean results.'],['Code example',`a = 10\nb = 3\nprint(a + b)\nprint(a / b)\nprint(a // b)\nprint(a % b)\nprint(a ** 2)\nprint(a > b)`]],output:'13\n3.3333333333333335\n3\n1\n100\nTrue',practice:'Build a simple bill calculator using price, quantity, and tax.',mistakes:'Do not confuse % with percentage; in Python it calculates a remainder. Use parentheses when an expression needs a clear order.'},
{title:'Strings',level:'Beginner',intro:'A string is a sequence of characters enclosed in single, double, or triple quotes. Strings support indexing, slicing, methods, and formatting.',sections:[['Indexing and slicing','Indexing starts at 0. text[0] gets the first character; text[1:4] gets a slice.'],['Useful methods','lower(), upper(), strip(), replace(), split(), and startswith() are common string methods.'],['Code example',`text = "  Python Programming  "\nprint(text.strip())\nprint(text.lower())\nprint(text[2:8])\nprint(text.replace("Programming", "Coding"))`]],output:'Python Programming\n  python programming  \nPython\n  Python Coding  ',practice:'Ask for a sentence and print it in uppercase, lowercase, and without extra spaces.',mistakes:'Strings are immutable: methods return a new string instead of changing the original automatically.'},
{title:'Conditional Statements',level:'Beginner',intro:'Conditional statements allow a program to make decisions. Python uses if, elif, and else with indentation to define each block.',sections:[['Decision flow','The if block runs when its condition is true. elif checks another condition, while else handles all remaining cases.'],['Combining conditions','Use and, or, and not to combine boolean expressions.'],['Code example',`marks = 78\n\nif marks >= 80:\n    grade = "A"\nelif marks >= 60:\n    grade = "B"\nelse:\n    grade = "Needs improvement"\n\nprint(grade)`]],output:'B',practice:'Ask for a number and tell whether it is positive, negative, or zero.',mistakes:'A colon is required after if, elif, and else. The statements inside a block must be indented.'},
{title:'For Loops',level:'Beginner',intro:'A for loop repeats code for each item in a sequence or iterable. It is useful when the number of items or repetitions is known.',sections:[['range()','range(stop) produces numbers from 0 up to stop - 1. range(start, stop, step) gives more control.'],['Looping over text','A for loop can visit each character in a string or each item in a list.'],['Code example',`for number in range(1, 6):\n    print(number)\n\nfor letter in "Py":\n    print(letter)`]],output:'1\n2\n3\n4\n5\nP\ny',practice:'Print the multiplication table of a number from 1 to 10.',mistakes:'The stop value in range() is excluded. Do not forget indentation inside the loop.'},
{title:'While Loops',level:'Beginner',intro:'A while loop repeats as long as its condition remains true. It is useful for input validation and situations where the end is not known in advance.',sections:[['Loop condition','The condition is checked before each repetition.'],['Updating state','Change a variable inside the loop so the condition eventually becomes false.'],['Code example',`count = 1\nwhile count <= 5:\n    print("Count:", count)\n    count += 1`]],output:'Count: 1\nCount: 2\nCount: 3\nCount: 4\nCount: 5',practice:'Keep asking the user for a password until the correct password is entered.',mistakes:'If the condition never becomes false, the loop may run forever. Always plan how the loop ends.'},
{title:'Lists',level:'Beginner',intro:'A list stores multiple values in an ordered, changeable collection. Lists can contain mixed types and are one of the most useful Python data structures.',sections:[['Creating and indexing','Use square brackets. Indexing starts at 0 and negative indexes count from the end.'],['Common methods','append(), extend(), insert(), remove(), pop(), sort(), and reverse() modify lists.'],['Code example',`fruits = ["apple", "banana", "mango"]\nfruits.append("orange")\nfruits[1] = "grape"\nprint(fruits)\nprint(len(fruits))`]],output:`['apple', 'grape', 'mango', 'orange']\n4`,practice:'Create a list of five subjects, add one subject, remove one, and print the result.',mistakes:'IndexError occurs when an index does not exist. append() adds one item, while extend() adds items from another iterable.'},
{title:'Tuples and Sets',level:'Intermediate',intro:'Tuples are ordered collections that cannot be changed after creation. Sets are unordered collections of unique values and are useful for removing duplicates and membership tests.',sections:[['Tuples','Create a tuple with parentheses or commas. Tuples are useful for fixed records.'],['Sets','Create a set with braces or set(). Sets automatically remove duplicate values.'],['Code example',`point = (10, 20)\ncolors = {"red", "blue", "red", "green"}\nprint(point[0])\nprint(colors)\nprint("blue" in colors)`]],output:'10\n{... unique colors ...}\nTrue',practice:'Create a tuple for a coordinate and a set containing repeated numbers. Display both.',mistakes:'An empty set is set(), not {} because {} creates an empty dictionary.'},
{title:'Dictionaries',level:'Intermediate',intro:'A dictionary stores data as key-value pairs. Keys are used to retrieve values quickly and should be unique and hashable.',sections:[['Creating a dictionary','Use braces with key: value pairs.'],['Reading and updating','Access values with data[key], or use get() when a key may be missing.'],['Code example',`student = {"name": "Hafiz", "age": 18, "course": "Python"}\nprint(student["name"])\nstudent["age"] = 19\nstudent["city"] = "Lahore"\nprint(student.get("email", "Not provided"))`]],output:'Hafiz\nNot provided',practice:'Create a dictionary for a book with title, author, and year, then update one value.',mistakes:'Accessing a missing key with [] raises KeyError. Use get() when a default is helpful.'},
{title:'Functions',level:'Intermediate',intro:'A function is a reusable block of code that performs a task. Functions improve organization, reduce repetition, and make programs easier to test.',sections:[['Defining a function','Use def, a name, parentheses, and a colon. The body is indented.'],['Parameters and return','Parameters receive input. return sends a result back to the caller.'],['Code example',`def add_numbers(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nresult = add_numbers(12, 8)\nprint(result)`]],output:'20',practice:'Write a function that accepts a number and returns its square.',mistakes:'Defining a function does not run it; call it by writing its name with parentheses. return is different from print.'},
{title:'Function Arguments and Scope',level:'Intermediate',intro:'Python supports positional arguments, keyword arguments, default values, and variable-length arguments. Scope determines where a variable can be accessed.',sections:[['Default and keyword arguments','Default values make arguments optional. Keyword arguments make calls clearer.'],['Local and global scope','A variable created inside a function is local unless you explicitly work with global state.'],['Code example',`def greet(name, message="Welcome"):\n    return f"{message}, {name}!"\n\nprint(greet("Hafiz"))\nprint(greet(name="Aisha", message="Hello"))`]],output:'Welcome, Hafiz!\nHello, Aisha!',practice:'Create a function with one required argument and one default argument.',mistakes:'Avoid changing global variables inside functions unless necessary. Keep functions focused.'},
{title:'List Comprehensions',level:'Intermediate',intro:'A list comprehension creates a new list from an iterable in one readable expression. It can include a transformation and an optional condition.',sections:[['Basic form','The pattern is [expression for item in iterable].'],['Adding a condition','Use [expression for item in iterable if condition].'],['Code example',`numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\nsquares = [n * n for n in numbers]\nevens = [n for n in numbers if n % 2 == 0]\nprint(squares)\nprint(evens)`]],output:'[1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36]\n[2, 4, 6]',practice:'Create a list of the lengths of all words in a sentence.',mistakes:'Do not make comprehensions so complex that a normal loop would be clearer.'},
{title:'Modules and Packages',level:'Intermediate',intro:'A module is a Python file containing reusable code. A package is a folder of related modules. Importing keeps programs organized and avoids rewriting common functionality.',sections:[['Importing','Use import module or from module import name.'],['Standard library','Python includes modules such as math, random, datetime, os, and pathlib.'],['Code example',`import math\nimport random\n\nprint(math.sqrt(81))\nprint(random.randint(1, 6))`]],output:'9.0\nA random integer from 1 to 6',practice:'Use the math module to calculate the area of a circle.',mistakes:'Do not name your own file random.py, math.py, or another standard module name.'},
{title:'File Handling',level:'Intermediate',intro:'File handling lets programs save and read information. Use open() with a context manager so the file is closed automatically.',sections:[['Modes','r reads, w writes and replaces, a appends, and x creates a new file.'],['with statement','The with block safely manages the file resource.'],['Code example',`with open("notes.txt", "w", encoding="utf-8") as file:\n    file.write("Python practice\n")\n\nwith open("notes.txt", "r", encoding="utf-8") as file:\n    content = file.read()\n    print(content)`]],output:'Python practice',practice:'Write three lines to a file and then read them back.',mistakes:'w overwrites existing content. Use a for loop or readlines() for large files instead of loading everything at once.'},
{title:'Exception Handling',level:'Intermediate',intro:'Exceptions are runtime errors that can be handled so a program fails gracefully. Use try, except, else, and finally when appropriate.',sections:[['Handling errors','Put risky code in try and handle expected errors in except.'],['else and finally','else runs when no exception occurs; finally runs whether an exception occurs or not.'],['Code example',`try:\n    number = int(input("Enter a number: "))\n    print(10 / number)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print("Please enter a whole number.")\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print("Zero cannot be used as a divisor.")`]],output:'A helpful message for invalid input or zero',practice:'Create a safe calculator that handles invalid numbers and division by zero.',mistakes:'Catch specific exceptions instead of using bare except everywhere. Do not hide errors silently.'},
{title:'Object-Oriented Programming: Classes',level:'Intermediate',intro:'Object-oriented programming models data and behavior together. A class is a blueprint, while an object is an instance created from that class.',sections:[['Class and object','Define a class with class. Create objects by calling the class name.'],['The __init__ method','__init__ initializes attributes when an object is created.'],['Code example',`class Student:\n    def __init__(self, name, course):\n        self.name = name\n        self.course = course\n\n    def introduce(self):\n        return f"I am {self.name} and I study {self.course}."\n\nstudent = Student("Hafiz", "Python")\nprint(student.introduce())`]],output:'I am Hafiz and I study Python.',practice:'Create a Car class with brand and model attributes and a method that describes the car.',mistakes:'Instance methods need self as their first parameter. Use self.attribute to access object data.'},
{title:'Inheritance and Polymorphism',level:'Advanced',intro:'Inheritance lets a child class reuse or extend a parent class. Polymorphism allows different objects to respond to the same method name in their own way.',sections:[['Inheritance','A child class can call parent behavior with super().'],['Overriding','A child can define a method with the same name to provide specialized behavior.'],['Code example',`class Animal:\n    def speak(self):\n        return "Some sound"\n\nclass Dog(Animal):\n    def speak(self):\n        return "Woof"\n\nanimals = [Animal(), Dog()]\nfor animal in animals:\n    print(animal.speak())`]],output:'Some sound\nWoof',practice:'Create a base Shape class and two child classes with their own area() methods.',mistakes:'Use inheritance when there is a genuine “is-a” relationship. Prefer composition when objects simply use other objects.'},
{title:'Iterators and Generators',level:'Advanced',intro:'An iterator produces values one at a time. A generator function uses yield to produce values lazily, which can save memory for large sequences.',sections:[['Iterable vs iterator','An iterable can provide an iterator through iter(). An iterator provides the next value with next().'],['Generators','A generator pauses at yield and resumes when the next value is requested.'],['Code example',`def countdown(start):\n    while start > 0:\n        yield start\n        start -= 1\n\nfor value in countdown(3):\n    print(value)`]],output:'3\n2\n1',practice:'Write a generator that yields the first five even numbers.',mistakes:'A generator is consumed as you iterate over it. Convert it to a list only when you need all values at once.'},
{title:'Decorators',level:'Advanced',intro:'A decorator is a function that wraps another function to add behavior without changing its original code. Decorators are common in logging, validation, web frameworks, and authorization.',sections:[['Core idea','A decorator receives a function and returns a new function.'],['@ syntax','Writing @decorator above a function is shorthand for function = decorator(function).'],['Code example',`def log_call(function):\n    def wrapper():\n        print("Function started")\n        function()\n        print("Function finished")\n    return wrapper\n\n@log_call\ndef say_hello():\n    print("Hello!")\n\nsay_hello()`]],output:'Function started\nHello!\nFunction finished',practice:'Create a decorator that prints a message before a function runs.',mistakes:'For decorators with arguments, use *args and **kwargs and preserve metadata with functools.wraps.'},
{title:'Regular Expressions',level:'Advanced',intro:'Regular expressions are patterns used to search, validate, and transform text. Python provides them through the re module.',sections:[['Patterns','Use literals, character classes, quantifiers, and anchors to describe text patterns.'],['Searching','re.search() finds a match, while re.findall() returns all matching pieces.'],['Code example',`import re\n\ntext = "Contact: student@example.com"\nmatch = re.search(r"[\\w.-]+@[\\w.-]+\\.\\w+", text)\nif match:\n    print(match.group())`]],output:'student@example.com',practice:'Find all numbers in a sentence using re.findall().',mistakes:'Raw strings such as r"\\d+" make backslashes easier to read. Validate real-world input carefully; regex is not always the best parser.'},
{title:'Virtual Environments and pip',level:'Advanced',intro:'A virtual environment isolates project dependencies. pip installs packages from the Python Package Index and helps reproduce a project setup.',sections:[['Create an environment','Run python -m venv .venv, then activate it according to your operating system.'],['Install packages','Use python -m pip install package-name. Save dependencies with pip freeze > requirements.txt.'],['Code example',`# After activating your virtual environment\npython -m pip install requests\n\n# Save installed dependencies\npython -m pip freeze > requirements.txt`]],output:'The package installation output from pip',practice:'Create a virtual environment for a practice project and install one safe learning package.',mistakes:'Activate the correct environment before installing. Do not commit the .venv folder to Git.'},
{title:'Working with JSON and APIs',level:'Advanced',intro:'JSON is a common text format for exchanging structured data. APIs allow programs to communicate with services over HTTP. The json module converts between Python objects and JSON text.',sections:[['JSON conversion','json.dumps() converts a Python object to a JSON string; json.loads() parses a JSON string.'],['Requests concept','An HTTP client such as requests can send a GET request to an API. Check status codes and handle errors.'],['Code example',`import json\n\ndata = {"name": "Hafiz", "skills": ["Python", "HTML"]}\njson_text = json.dumps(data, indent=2)\nprint(json_text)\n\nrestored = json.loads(json_text)\nprint(restored["skills"][0])`]],output:'A formatted JSON object\nPython',practice:'Convert a dictionary to JSON and then convert it back to a dictionary.',mistakes:'Never assume an API response is valid JSON. Check errors, timeouts, and whether you are allowed to use the service.'},
{title:'SQLite Databases',level:'Advanced',intro:'SQLite is a lightweight database included with Python. It stores structured data in a file and is useful for small applications, prototypes, and learning SQL.',sections:[['Connection and table','Use sqlite3.connect() and execute SQL statements through a cursor.'],['CRUD operations','Create, read, update, and delete are the four basic data operations.'],['Code example',`import sqlite3\n\nconnection = sqlite3.connect("school.db")\ncur = connection.cursor()\ncur.execute("CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS students (name TEXT, age INTEGER)")\ncur.execute("INSERT INTO students VALUES (?, ?)", ("Hafiz", 18))\nconnection.commit()\n\nfor row in cur.execute("SELECT * FROM students"):\n    print(row)\nconnection.close()`]],output:"('Hafiz', 18)",practice:'Create a SQLite table for books and insert two records using parameterized queries.',mistakes:'Use placeholders (?) instead of building SQL with string concatenation. Close connections or use context managers.'},
{title:'Testing with unittest',level:'Advanced',intro:'Testing checks whether code behaves as expected. Automated tests make refactoring safer and help catch regressions early.',sections:[['Test structure','A test case groups related checks. Each test method commonly begins with test_.'],['Assertions','assertEqual, assertTrue, assertRaises, and related methods express expected behavior.'],['Code example',`import unittest\n\ndef add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nclass TestMath(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_add(self):\n        self.assertEqual(add(2, 3), 5)\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    unittest.main()`]],output:'A successful test result such as OK',practice:'Write two tests for a function that checks whether a number is even.',mistakes:'Test normal cases and edge cases. A test that never fails may not be testing meaningful behavior.'},
{title:'Project Structure and Best Practices',level:'Advanced',intro:'Professional Python projects use clear structure, meaningful names, documentation, dependency files, tests, and version control. Clean code is easier for others and your future self to understand.',sections:[['A simple structure','Separate application code, tests, documentation, and configuration.'],['PEP 8 basics','Use four spaces for indentation, snake_case for functions and variables, and descriptive names.'],['Code example',`def calculate_total(price, quantity):\n    """Return the total price for a quantity of items."""\n    if quantity < 0:\n        raise ValueError("quantity cannot be negative")\n    return price * quantity\n\nprint(calculate_total(250, 3))`]],output:'750',practice:'Choose one earlier exercise and refactor it into functions with clear names and a short docstring.',mistakes:'Avoid giant files and duplicated code. Do not optimize before measuring; first make code correct and readable.'},
{title:'Capstone Project: Build a CLI Student Manager',level:'Advanced',intro:'A capstone combines input, conditions, loops, functions, lists or dictionaries, file storage, and error handling into one practical application. Build it in small, testable steps.',sections:[['Project goals','Create, list, search, and remove student records. Store data in JSON or SQLite so it remains after the program closes.'],['Suggested plan','Start with a menu loop, create one function per action, validate input, and save after changes.'],['Starter code',`students = []\n\ndef add_student():\n    name = input("Student name: ").strip()\n    if name:\n        students.append({"name": name})\n        print("Student added.")\n\ndef show_students():\n    if not students:\n        print("No students yet.")\n        return\n    for index, student in enumerate(students, start=1):\n        print(index, student["name"])\n\nwhile True:\n    print("\\n1. Add  2. List  3. Exit")\n    choice = input("Choose: ")\n    if choice == "1":\n        add_student()\n    elif choice == "2":\n        show_students()\n    elif choice == "3":\n        break\n    else:\n        print("Invalid choice")`]],output:'A menu-driven student manager that lets you add and list students.',practice:'Add search, delete, JSON saving, and JSON loading. Then write tests for the data functions.',mistakes:'Build one feature at a time. Validate user input and keep storage code separate from menu code.'}
];

let state={page:'courses',user:null,selected:null,selectedLesson:0,query:'',category:'All courses',theme:'light'};
let aiMessages=[]; let aiBusy=false;
window.state=state;
const $=s=>document.querySelector(s);
function brand(){return `<div class="brand"><div class="brandmark">&lt;/&gt;</div><div>CodewithHafiz<small>LEARN. BUILD. GROW.</small></div></div>`}
function header(){return `<header class="topbar">${brand()}<nav class="nav"><button class="${state.page==='lab'?'active':''}" onclick="go('lab')">Code Lab</button><button class="${state.page==='courses'?'active':''}" onclick="go('courses')">Courses</button><button class="${state.page==='tutorials'?'active':''}" onclick="go('tutorials')">Tutorials</button><button class="${state.page==='learning'||state.page==='lesson'?'active':''}" onclick="go('learning')">My learning</button><button class="${state.page==='setup'?'active':''}" onclick="go('setup')">Downloads</button></nav><div class="top-actions"><button class="ghost" onclick="toggleTheme()">${state.theme==='light'?'☾':'☀'} ${state.theme==='light'?'Dark':'Light'}</button>${state.user?`<button class="ghost" onclick="go('dashboard')">Dashboard ↗</button><div class="avatar">${state.user[0].toUpperCase()}</div><button class="ghost" onclick="logout()">↪</button>`:`<button class="pill" onclick="go('login')">Sign in</button>`}</div></header>`}
function home(){let list=courses.filter(c=>(state.category==='All courses'||c.category===state.category)&&(`${c.title} ${c.desc}`.toLowerCase().includes(state.query.toLowerCase())));return `${header()}<main class="container"><section class="hero"><div><div class="eyebrow">KEEP YOUR CURIOSITY. BUILD YOUR SKILLS.</div><h1>Your next chapter<br>starts with <span>code.</span></h1><p>Practical courses. Clear explanations. Real progress.<br>Start from scratch or pick up where you left off.</p></div><div class="code-card"><div class="code-head"><span>your_journey.py</span><span>⌁</span></div><div class="code-body"><div class="comment"># A little progress, every day</div><br><span class="green">while</span> curious:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;learn()<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;build()<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="green">grow()</span><br><br><span class="comment"># Your future is a work in progress.</span></div></div></section><div class="benefits"><span>${courses.length} free courses</span><span>Learn by doing</span><span>Beginner friendly</span><span>Go at your own pace</span></div><section><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">FIND YOUR STARTING POINT</div><h2>Explore courses</h2></div><input class="search" placeholder="⌕  What do you want to learn?" value="${state.query}" oninput="state.query=this.value;render()"></div><div class="filters">${['All courses','Programming','Web Development','DevOps','Developer Tools','Data & AI'].map(x=>`<button class="filter ${state.category===x?'active':''}" onclick="state.category='${x}';render()">${x}</button>`).join('')}</div><div class="course-grid">${list.map(courseCard).join('')}</div></section><section class="setup"><div><div class="eyebrow">READY TO START</div><h2>Download your coding tools.</h2><p>Install Python, Visual Studio Code, and the official Python extension to begin coding.</p><div class="download-mini-grid"><a class="download-mini" href="https://www.python.org/downloads/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>🐍 Python</b><span>Download Python ↗</span></a><a class="download-mini" href="https://code.visualstudio.com/download" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>💻 VS Code</b><span>Download VS Code ↗</span></a><a class="download-mini" href="https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ms-python.python" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>🧩 Python Extension</b><span>Install extension ↗</span></a></div></div><button onclick="go('setup')">Open full setup guide →</button></section></main>`}
